### What is the Backspace String Compare problem?

Given two strings that may contain `#` characters representing backspaces, determine whether they are equal after all backspaces are applied. For example, `"ab#c"` produces `"ac"` and `"ad#c"` also produces `"ac"`, so they are equal.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BackspaceCompare {
    // Stack-based approach (O(n) space)
    public static boolean compareStack(String s, String t) {
        return build(s).equals(build(t));
    }

    private static String build(String s) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == '#') {
                if (sb.length() > 0) sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);
            } else {
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(compareStack("ab#c", "ad#c")); // true
        System.out.println(compareStack("ab##", "c#d#")); // true
        System.out.println(compareStack("a#c", "b"));     // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
```

### How does the constant-space two-pointer approach work?

A backspace deletes characters to its left, so reading right to left lets you account for every deletion as you encounter it. Place a pointer at the end of each string. For each string, use a skip counter: when you see `#`, increment skip and move left; when you see a normal character with positive skip, decrement skip and move left (the character is consumed); stop when you reach a surviving character (skip is zero and the character is not `#`).

Compare the two surviving characters. If they differ, the strings are not equal. If both pointers run off the start together, the strings are equal.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BackspaceTwoPointers {
    public static boolean compare(String s, String t) {
        int i = s.length() - 1;
        int j = t.length() - 1;

        while (i >= 0 || j >= 0) {
            i = nextValid(s, i);
            j = nextValid(t, j);

            if (i >= 0 && j >= 0) {
                if (s.charAt(i) != t.charAt(j)) return false;
            } else if (i >= 0 || j >= 0) {
                return false; // one has chars left, the other does not
            }
            i--;
            j--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private static int nextValid(String s, int idx) {
        int skip = 0;
        while (idx >= 0) {
            if (s.charAt(idx) == '#') { skip++; idx--; }
            else if (skip > 0) { skip--; idx--; }
            else break;
        }
        return idx;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(compare("ab#c", "ad#c")); // true
        System.out.println(compare("ab##", "c#d#")); // true
        System.out.println(compare("a#c", "b"));     // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
```

### Why scan from right to left?

A backspace affects characters to its left, not to its right. If you scan left to right, you do not know whether the current character will be deleted by a future backspace until you have read the rest of the string. Scanning right to left means you encounter backspaces before the characters they delete, so you can skip the correct characters immediately.

### How does the skip counter work?

The skip counter tracks pending backspaces. Each `#` increments it. Each normal character with a positive skip counter is consumed (deleted) and decrements the counter. The helper stops when it finds a normal character with skip equal to zero, which is a surviving character.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SkipCounterTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "abc##d";
        int idx = s.length() - 1;
        int skip = 0;

        while (idx >= 0) {
            char c = s.charAt(idx);
            if (c == '#') {
                skip++;
                System.out.println("idx=" + idx + " '#' skip=" + skip);
            } else if (skip > 0) {
                skip--;
                System.out.println("idx=" + idx + " '" + c + "' consumed, skip=" + skip);
            } else {
                System.out.println("idx=" + idx + " '" + c + "' survives");
            }
            idx--;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
idx=5 'd' survives
idx=4 '#' skip=1
idx=3 '#' skip=2
idx=2 'c' consumed, skip=1
idx=1 'b' consumed, skip=0
idx=0 'a' survives
```

### What is the tricky termination condition?

The two strings can have different lengths and different numbers of backspaces, so the pointers rarely move in sync. After each skip, one pointer might be off the start while the other still points at a surviving character. This means the final strings differ in length. The comparison must handle the case where only one pointer is still valid.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TerminationEdge {
    public static boolean compare(String s, String t) {
        int i = s.length() - 1, j = t.length() - 1;
        while (i >= 0 || j >= 0) {
            i = skip(s, i); j = skip(t, j);
            if (i >= 0 && j >= 0) { if (s.charAt(i) != t.charAt(j)) return false; }
            else if (i >= 0 || j >= 0) return false; // one still has chars
            i--; j--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private static int skip(String s, int idx) {
        int sk = 0;
        while (idx >= 0) {
            if (s.charAt(idx) == '#') { sk++; idx--; }
            else if (sk > 0) { sk--; idx--; }
            else break;
        }
        return idx;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // "a" after backspaces vs "" after backspaces
        System.out.println(compare("a", "b#")); // false ("a" vs "")
    }
}
```

```text
false
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include empty strings, strings that are entirely backspaces, strings where backspaces outnumber characters, and strings with consecutive backspaces.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BackspaceEdgeCases {
    public static boolean compare(String s, String t) {
        int i = s.length() - 1, j = t.length() - 1;
        while (i >= 0 || j >= 0) {
            i = skip(s, i); j = skip(t, j);
            if (i >= 0 && j >= 0) { if (s.charAt(i) != t.charAt(j)) return false; }
            else if (i >= 0 || j >= 0) return false;
            i--; j--;
        }
        return true;
    }
    private static int skip(String s, int idx) {
        int sk = 0;
        while (idx >= 0) {
            if (s.charAt(idx) == '#') { sk++; idx--; }
            else if (sk > 0) { sk--; idx--; }
            else break;
        }
        return idx;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Both empty: " + compare("", ""));       // true
        System.out.println("All backspace: " + compare("###", "##")); // true (both empty)
        System.out.println("Extra backspace: " + compare("a###", "")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
Both empty: true
All backspace: true
Extra backspace: true
```

### How do the two approaches compare?

| Feature | Stack/StringBuilder | Two-pointer (right to left) |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(n + m) | O(n + m) |
| Space | O(n + m) | O(1) |
| Clarity | Very simple | More complex |
| When to use | Default choice | When constant space required |

The stack approach is easier to get right and is perfectly acceptable unless the interviewer specifically asks for constant space.

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, a legacy entry terminal records every keystroke including corrections where `#` represents the backspace key. During reconciliation, Rajesh must decide whether two operators ended up entering the same final transaction reference code even though their raw keystroke logs look different due to corrections. The logs are long and run on memory-constrained infrastructure, so the right-to-left two-pointer comparison does it in constant space.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class KeystrokeReconciliation {
    public static boolean sameResult(String log1, String log2) {
        int i = log1.length() - 1, j = log2.length() - 1;
        while (i >= 0 || j >= 0) {
            i = skip(log1, i); j = skip(log2, j);
            if (i >= 0 && j >= 0) { if (log1.charAt(i) != log2.charAt(j)) return false; }
            else if (i >= 0 || j >= 0) return false;
            i--; j--;
        }
        return true;
    }
    private static int skip(String s, int idx) {
        int sk = 0;
        while (idx >= 0) {
            if (s.charAt(idx) == '#') { sk++; idx--; }
            else if (sk > 0) { sk--; idx--; }
            else break;
        }
        return idx;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String rajeshLog = "SAV20#01";
        String priyaLog = "SAV201#1";
        System.out.println("Same result: " + sameResult(rajeshLog, priyaLog));
    }
}
```

```text
Same result: true
```
